#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119480 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3ff1822c-afad-54f8-aa36-7e0de79d9d04" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Unit Tangent Vector at a Given Point"}] :question/id #uuid "c44c0973-5253-50c5-98b1-15938b79494e" :question/math-academy-id "e-3054" :question/problem "What is the unit tangent vector to the curve ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14050604/assets/1a5a0925f4e15e00c9eff4bb4f39ce9c6eda232881bbddb07184ac54bbc269a8.png) at the point ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14050604/assets/1e24125540d8b43cf8d314684fef6fb5be15c62d6988f9aac984f9812e6a74fd.png)" :question/worked-solution "For a curve ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14050604/assets/d7ec57597ee1ec3ed5d675c6d1cfde0115309f037e92d4aae963be1e54c97173.png) the unit tangent vector ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14050604/assets/beaa6501c934b5c7325c3b3385bae9fcc0388a74d5983995c83f4d8813e93f06.png) at the point ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14050604/assets/5313a9faaa4dde443c2eb79dedc7d917f6640579d0f038ffaf045ed01fd465f4.png) is given by \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14050604/assets/ff0324d951fb404b12736c08195532ce8d2fe57cae5baf845673b3626b871eb6.png) \n\nFirst, we find the value of ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14050604/assets/7a5dc70352bd991ad75358cc62cef8d67afbb5cb00da1578f008a0b392d2385d.png) that corresponds to the point ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14050604/assets/83fbafba1d5d5be0d132254724f1cadcd31c51dc923c63113d0f393bf6bf94bc.png) on the given curve. We know that\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14050604/assets/aebba9c47fe0ea053b4fc1924e7f3a97ee17dc60fc074091ba0f6c303301cfda.png)\nIn the above equation, the first component in each of the two vectors is equal to ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14050604/assets/549c2a20cf7ec79cb4b26e39d950105c670a74452b2d7d024d3d5e64d553ecbe.png) Equating the other two components gives the system\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14050604/assets/187d4f3cd4d27fdac888ccf0d88c58f0cfb073913999f5767584476d9d2d4e10.png)\nFrom the first equation, ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14050604/assets/02ad803439ddfa163c4788fb00ff86c65d3e0dc0711af87290695a8f06471e81.png) This value satisfies the second equation too. Therefore, the point ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14050604/assets/3d4f13f8fb24239874d83180ba75954be6d86ec8bb6e339bea3a0c1c9a4edae4.png) is at the tip of ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14050604/assets/7479ebb2b1c8ed8146f6833341d2e732dcd91f86781823d36906dd19cadca82c.png) \n\nNow, we calculate ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14050604/assets/2a03e92cf496f1508de5344917e1f5fe5574f9801a0ef229649288a29c15044b.png) and evaluate it at ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14050604/assets/06f646a39990b7ed47b7ab431b54ea320a05650e5e4f6df6f51812460536154b.png)\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14050604/assets/9432511f61bf63030d95771cdc107682a61444fe925dab681ab8388b4a48524e.png) \n\nNext, we calculate the magnitude of ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14050604/assets/3b10ec5094240cfd7f221bc22e19a29d74b4a69d2788887265041cc7db5291ca.png)\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14050604/assets/ff9d6534ce414a2151a6dbbe2d407328ed6514447608f8601dfed6a1de27471c.png)\n\nFinally, we normalize ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14050604/assets/e9ece600ee31f7dc6e24c0639da455b0bba4fc3a7765986b08473109a93f0883.png) and obtain the unit tangent vector to the given curve at ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14050604/assets/5deae1091d1cd3c2502da1214919e0520c8cbe620d0ae3c2f1f926caf52f3cc0.png)\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14050604/assets/f110fc18608eb66b7f1927adb60049b76390669485638da4cddd592e23738998.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119481 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c400d85b-94d5-5fd2-98b4-472ab43c5be4" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Unit Tangent Vector at an Arbitrary Point"}] :question/id #uuid "95ba9167-9e49-557d-bfbd-89bdeec8939f" :question/math-academy-id "e-3055" :question/problem "Find the unit tangent vector to the curve $\\mathbf r(t)=\\left\\langle \\sin^3{t}, \\:\\cos^3{t} \\right\\rangle$ at an arbitrary point where $0 < t < \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "For a curve $\\mathbf r(t)= x(t) \\,\\mathbf i + y(t) \\,\\mathbf j,$ the unit tangent vector $\\mathbf T(t)$ at the point $P(x(t),y(t))$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\mathbf T(t)=\\dfrac{\\mathbf r'(t)}{\\left\\| \\mathbf r'(t) \\right\\|}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the derivative of $\\mathbf r(t)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({sin}^{3}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({cos}^{3}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3{sin}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,3{cos}^{2}⁡t(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the magnitude of $\\mathbf r'(t)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =|3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t|\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡t+(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\ \\text{ } & =|3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t|\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =|3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t|\\sqrt{1} \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the last step, we were able to remove the absolute value bars because $\\sin{t}>0$ and $\\cos{t}>0$ for $0 < t < \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}.$\n\nFinally, we normalize $\\mathbf r'(t)$ and obtain the unit tangent vector to the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(t) & =\\frac{{r}^{′}(t)}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩}{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t} \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119482 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "34342124-8cee-5b70-bf57-64f746af8912" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Unit Tangent Vectors Using the Rules of Differentiation For Vector Functions"}] :question/id #uuid "bb704e8f-1fab-50c0-b768-c50c729d4e45" :question/math-academy-id "e-3056" :question/problem "Find the unit tangent vector to the curve $\\mathbf r(t)=e^{2t}\\mathbf p(t)$ at the point where $t=0,$ if $\\mathbf p(0)=\\mathbf i-\\,\\mathbf j-\\mathbf k$ and $\\mathbf p'(0)=\\mathbf i - \\mathbf j+ 2\\mathbf k.$" :question/worked-solution "For a curve $\\mathbf r(t)=x(t)\\mathbf i+y(t)\\mathbf j+z(t)\\mathbf k,$ the unit tangent vector $\\mathbf T(t)$ at the point $P(x(t),y(t),z(t))$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf T(t)=\\dfrac{\\mathbf r'(t)}{\\left\\| \\mathbf r'(t) \\right\\|}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find $\\mathbf r'(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=0\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{2t}p(t)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{2t})p(t)+{e}^{2t}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(p(t)) \\\\  & =2{e}^{2t}p(t)+{e}^{2t}{p}^{′}(t) \\\\ {r}^{′}(0) & =2{e}^{2(0)}p(0)+{e}^{2(0)}{p}^{′}(0) \\\\  & =2(i-\\,j-k)+1(i-j+2k) \\\\  & =3\\,i-3\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we calculate the magnitude of $\\mathbf r'(0)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}^{′}(0)‖ & =\\sqrt{(3)^{2}+(-3)^{2}+(0)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2\\cdot {3}^{2}} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize $\\mathbf r'(0)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(0) & =\\frac{{r}^{′}(0)}{‖{r}^{′}(0)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}}(3\\,i-3\\,j) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}(i-j) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(i-j)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the unit tangent vector to the curve at the point that corresponds to $t=0$ is $\\mathbf T(0) = \\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(\\mathbf i -  \\mathbf j).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329369 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c400d85b-94d5-5fd2-98b4-472ab43c5be4" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Unit Tangent Vector at an Arbitrary Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e56f8f54-bfa8-5ed9-80d3-b0bbc21bbeda" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+4t}}(i-\\,j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329371} {:answer/id #uuid "d625ce53-3f65-5483-bff7-7c35b855dfb3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+4t}}(i-2\\sqrt{t}\\,j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329372} {:answer/id #uuid "db57f7ca-962f-50e6-a503-551e18b29a64" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+4t}}(\\sqrt{t}\\,i-2t\\,j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329373} {:answer/id #uuid "d8e669c5-474c-54ec-9b1a-57663e4d586d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{1+4t}(i-\\sqrt{t}\\,j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329374} {:answer/id #uuid "4ea4ea58-e74e-5dcb-b2a7-6271320606bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{1+4t}(3i+2\\sqrt{t}\\,j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329375}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d625ce53-3f65-5483-bff7-7c35b855dfb3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+4t}}(i-2\\sqrt{t}\\,j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329372} :answer-field/id #uuid "63eb97a8-40b9-53b8-a175-099d9f9a01c1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329370}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1cfc1910-4d17-5781-a2d7-34d0eb0a5a61" :question/math-academy-id "q-127213" :question/problem "Find the unit tangent vector to the curve $r(t)=\\sqrt{t}\\,i+(2-t)\\,j$ at an arbitrary point where $t\\in (0,\\infty ).$" :question/worked-solution "For a curve $r(t)=x(t)i+y(t)j$, the unit tangent vector $T(t)$ at the point $P(x(t),y(t))$ is given by\n\n$$\nT(t)=\\frac{{r}^{′}(t)}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the derivative of $r(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{t})\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2-t)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}}\\,i-\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the magnitude of ${r}^{′}(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}})}^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{4t}+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1+4t}{4t}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{1+4t}}{2\\sqrt{t}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize ${r}^{′}(t)$ and obtain the unit tangent vector to the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(t) & =\\frac{{r}^{′}(t)}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{t}}{\\sqrt{1+4t}}(\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}}i-\\,j) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+4t}}(i-2\\sqrt{t}\\,j)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120163 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "34342124-8cee-5b70-bf57-64f746af8912" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Unit Tangent Vectors Using the Rules of Differentiation For Vector Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a2e6a322-bfe3-5866-9e00-ec11064f82f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5}j + \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117499} {:answer/id #uuid "a8354a80-f3ad-53fb-a079-199fa7ca8aab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5}j - \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117500} {:answer/id #uuid "ae30b1c7-8c5a-5859-9ebc-63f23d7e1aee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5}j - \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117501} {:answer/id #uuid "0a13a4c6-ec2f-5f19-a257-25fc60c3552c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5}i - \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117502} {:answer/id #uuid "5eb5a3f8-58a4-5acd-869a-bce3409cf5ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5}i - \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117503}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a8354a80-f3ad-53fb-a079-199fa7ca8aab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5}j - \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117500} :answer-field/id #uuid "ca42adc8-9c03-57d4-8619-2101dd0bb0ca" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117498}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ecacb100-aa15-51ca-8b52-7438cb895ac6" :question/math-academy-id "q-33096" :question/problem "Find the unit tangent vector to the curve $r(t)={e}^{-t}p(t)$ at the point where $t=0$, if $p(0)=\\,i+\\,j+k$ and ${p}^{′}(0)=\\,i-\\,j.$" :question/worked-solution "For a curve $r(t)=x(t)i+y(t)j+z(t)k$, the unit tangent vector $T(t)$ at the point $P(x(t),y(t),z(t))$ is given by\n\n$$\nT(t)=\\frac{{r}^{′}(t)}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find ${r}^{′}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{-t}p(t)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{-t})p(t)+{e}^{-t}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(p(t)) \\\\  & =-{e}^{-t}p(t)+{e}^{-t}{p}^{′}(t) \\\\ {r}^{′}(0) & =-{e}^{0}p(0)+{e}^{0}{p}^{′}(0) \\\\  & =-(\\,i+\\,j+k)+(\\,i-\\,j) \\\\  & =-2j-k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the magnitude of ${r}^{′}(0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}^{′}(0)‖ & =\\sqrt{(0)^{2}+(-2)^{2}+(1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{0+4+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize ${r}^{′}(0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(0) & =\\frac{{r}^{′}(0)}{‖{r}^{′}(0)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}(-2j-k) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5}j-\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the unit tangent vector to the curve at the point that corresponds to $t=0$ is $T(0)=-\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5}j-\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}k.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329404 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3ff1822c-afad-54f8-aa36-7e0de79d9d04" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Unit Tangent Vector at a Given Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6b18d4b5-1c0a-5832-9020-771152a05bc8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{12}},\\,0,\\,\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{12}}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329406} {:answer/id #uuid "0b8f1921-7e69-575d-9872-5ca8b7b3cd4b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-\\frac{1}{2},\\,0,\\,\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329407} {:answer/id #uuid "e39dc574-480b-57d5-99b8-fd769235cb07" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{12}},\\,0,\\,\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{12}}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329408} {:answer/id #uuid "72d9a5aa-94cf-501a-944c-9714c3e9745d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{7}},\\,0,\\,\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{7}}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329409} {:answer/id #uuid "6f6cdb16-97a3-5d3d-9c7f-3add56eab9cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{1}{2},\\,0,\\,\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329410}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0b8f1921-7e69-575d-9872-5ca8b7b3cd4b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-\\frac{1}{2},\\,0,\\,\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329407} :answer-field/id #uuid "eb6615ec-89c0-5f31-b71b-be380265d7c3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329405}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f9509fd3-fbb9-5924-93c0-2012029b0bc2" :question/math-academy-id "q-33136" :question/problem "Find the unit tangent vector to the curve $r(t)=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}{t}^{2}⟩$ at the point where $t=\\pi .$" :question/worked-solution "For a curve $r(t)=⟨x(t),y(t),z(t)⟩$, the unit tangent vector $T(t)$ at the point $P(x(t),y(t),z(t))$ is given by\n\n$$\nT(t)=\\frac{{r}^{′}(t)}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find ${r}^{′}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=\\pi :$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}{t}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\cdot 2t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\sqrt{3}t⟩ \\\\ {r}^{′}(\\pi ) & =⟨\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi ,\\,\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi ,\\,\\sqrt{3}\\pi ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\pi ,\\,0,\\,\\sqrt{3}\\pi ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the magnitude of ${r}^{′}(\\pi ):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}^{′}(\\pi )‖ & =\\sqrt{(-\\pi )^{2}+(0)^{2}+(\\sqrt{3}\\pi )^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{\\pi }^{2}} \\\\  & =2\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize ${r}^{′}(\\pi )$ and obtain the unit tangent vector to the given curve at the point where $t=\\pi :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(\\pi ) & =\\frac{{r}^{′}(\\pi )}{‖{r}^{′}(\\pi )‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\pi }⟨-\\pi ,\\,0,\\,\\sqrt{3}\\pi ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{1}{2},\\,0,\\,\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329418 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3ff1822c-afad-54f8-aa36-7e0de79d9d04" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Unit Tangent Vector at a Given Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c3a6d6fc-9abd-5b11-9c4e-756f1ac99a06" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\,i+\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}}\\,j+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329420} {:answer/id #uuid "57ebf62e-a5e2-5fe0-9ee2-57a47167f5f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}\\,i+\\frac{2}{3}\\,j+\\frac{1}{3}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329421} {:answer/id #uuid "3593da56-4529-5477-8995-4d5dfea4bf6b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,i+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329422} {:answer/id #uuid "5183eaa4-7c6b-5c51-9da4-706cc8d3f980" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}}\\,i+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\,j+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329423} {:answer/id #uuid "3d3cf64b-53c5-5601-9bf2-57e7b73a12ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,i-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329424}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "57ebf62e-a5e2-5fe0-9ee2-57a47167f5f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}\\,i+\\frac{2}{3}\\,j+\\frac{1}{3}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329421} :answer-field/id #uuid "7c156753-5ae3-5a89-86d7-afe1d181da84" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329419}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "93a1c03f-77b3-5fa3-b1a1-22418386cc31" :question/math-academy-id "q-33584" :question/problem "What is the unit tangent vector to the curve $r(t)=2t\\,i+{t}^{2}\\,j+t\\,k$ at the point $P(2,1,1)?$" :question/worked-solution "For a curve $r(t)=x(t)\\,j+y(t)\\,j+z(t)\\,k$, the unit tangent vector $T(t)$ at the point $P(x(t),y(t),z(t))$ is given by \n\n$$\nT(t)=\\frac{{r}^{′}(t)}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the value of $t$ that corresponds to the point $P(2,1,1)$ on the given curve. We know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =2\\,i+\\,j+\\,k \\\\ 2t\\,i+{t}^{2}\\,j+t\\,k & =2\\,i+\\,j+\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the respective components gives the system\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}2t=2 \\\\ {t}^{2}=1 \\\\ t=1.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equality, $t=1.$ This value satisfies the other two equations. Therefore, the point $P(2,1,1)$ is at the tip of $r(1).$ \n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=1:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\,k \\\\  & =2\\,i+2t\\,j+\\,k \\\\ {r}^{′}(1) & =2\\,i+2(1)\\,j+\\,k \\\\  & =2\\,i+2\\,j+\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the magnitude of ${r}^{′}(1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}^{′}(1)‖ & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{2}^{2}+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9} \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize ${r}^{′}(1)$ and obtain the unit tangent vector to the given curve at $P(2,1,1):$  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(1) & =\\frac{{r}^{′}(1)}{‖{r}^{′}(1)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(2\\,i+2\\,j+\\,k) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\,i+\\frac{2}{3}\\,j+\\frac{1}{3}\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329432 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3ff1822c-afad-54f8-aa36-7e0de79d9d04" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Unit Tangent Vector at a Given Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "20e24c61-3ca5-5059-959b-69294f045281" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{1}{3},\\,\\frac{2}{3},\\,\\frac{2}{3}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329434} {:answer/id #uuid "7a526db6-2810-5253-921f-d0e056df39de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{2}{3},\\,\\frac{2}{3},\\,\\frac{1}{3}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329435} {:answer/id #uuid "dcb9aca5-f9a6-5f6a-9719-7d7f257828c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{2}{3},\\,\\frac{1}{3},\\,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329436} {:answer/id #uuid "775af2b0-5896-596a-ac26-3a36e7e6ea2c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{2}{3},\\,\\frac{1}{3},\\,\\frac{2}{3}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329437} {:answer/id #uuid "1d7f4363-8319-5a60-8ae9-e88f9bbe6f29" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}},\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}},\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329438}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "775af2b0-5896-596a-ac26-3a36e7e6ea2c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{2}{3},\\,\\frac{1}{3},\\,\\frac{2}{3}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329437} :answer-field/id #uuid "a57e84c6-5d42-5f67-9ff0-52b8d0eadf8b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329433}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c506b7f3-325f-50a6-8547-719d5dedc2ec" :question/math-academy-id "q-33655" :question/problem "What is the unit tangent vector to the curve $r(t)=⟨{t}^{4},\\,{t}^{2},\\,4t⟩$ at the point $P(1,1,4)?$" :question/worked-solution "For a curve $r(t)=⟨x(t),y(t),z(t)⟩$, the unit tangent vector $T(t)$ at the point $P(x(t),y(t),z(t))$ is given by\n\n$$\nT(t)=\\frac{{r}^{′}(t)}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the value of $t$ that corresponds to the point $P(1,1,4)$ on the given curve. We know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨1,\\,1,\\,4⟩ \\\\ ⟨{t}^{4},\\,{t}^{2},\\,4t⟩ & =⟨1,\\,1,\\,4⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the respective components gives the system\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}{t}^{4}=1 \\\\ {t}^{2}=1 \\\\ 4t=4.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the third equality, we get $t=1.$ This value satisfies the other two equations. Therefore, the point $P(1,1,4)$ is at the tip of $r(1).$  \n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{4}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4{t}^{3},\\,2t,\\,4⟩ \\\\ {r}^{′}(1) & =⟨4(1)^{3},\\,2(1),\\,4⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4,\\,2,\\,4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the magnitude of ${r}^{′}(1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}^{′}(1)‖ & =\\sqrt{{4}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{4}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{36} \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize ${r}^{′}(1)$ and obtain the unit tangent vector to the given curve at $P(1,1,4):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(1) & =\\frac{{r}^{′}(1)}{‖{r}^{′}(1)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}⟨4,\\,2,\\,4⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{2}{3},\\,\\frac{1}{3},\\,\\frac{2}{3}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
